We have that
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i &= 96, \\
\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^2 &= 144, \\
\sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^3 &= 216.
\end{align*}
Let $S_k = \sum_{i = 1}^n a_i^k$ for $k = 1, 2, 3$. Then we have
\begin{align*}
S_1 &= 96, \\
S_2 &= 144, \\
S_3 &= 216.
\end{align*}
By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have
\begin{align*}
(S_1)^2 &\geq nS_2, \\
(S_2)^2 &\geq nS_3.
\end{align*}
Thus,
\begin{align*}
96^2 &\geq n \cdot 144, \\
144^2 &\geq n \cdot 216.
\end{align*}
Dividing the first inequality by $144$ and the second inequality by $216$, we get
\begin{align*}
\frac{96^2}{144} &\geq n, \\
\frac{144^2}{216} &\leq n.
\end{align*}
Thus,
\begin{align*}
\frac{96^2}{144} &\leq n \leq \frac{144^2}{216}.
\end{align*}
Since $n$ is an integer, we have
\begin{align*}
\frac{96^2}{144} &\leq n \leq \frac{144^2}{216}.
\end{align*}
Thus, the possible values of $n$ are
\begin{align*}
\frac{96^2}{144} &\leq n \leq \frac{144^2}{216}.
\end{align*}
Thus, the sum of all possible values of $n$ is
\begin{align*}
\frac{96^2}{144} + \frac{144^2}{216}.
\end{align*}
Thus, the answer is $\boxed{160}$.
